_No response_

## Version
Vert.x 5.1.0 (regression introduced between 5.0.12 and 5.1.0).

## Summary
`io.vertx.core.net.TcpClientConfig#setConnectTimeout(Duration)` rejects
`Duration.ZERO` with `IllegalArgumentException: "connectTimeout must be >= 0"`.
The error text says `>= 0`, but the predicate is `isNegative() || isZero()` —
they disagree.

## Reproducer
```java
Vertx vertx = Vertx.vertx();
vertx.createHttpClient(new HttpClientOptions().setConnectTimeout(0));
// java.lang.IllegalArgumentException: connectTimeout must be >= 0
//   at io.vertx.core.net.TcpClientConfig.setConnectTimeout(TcpClientConfig.java:119)
//   at io.vertx.core.net.TcpClientConfig.<init>(TcpClientConfig.java:68)
//   at io.vertx.core.http.HttpClientConfig.<init>(HttpClientConfig.java:129)
//   at io.vertx.core.Vertx.createHttpClient(Vertx.java:468)
```

## Expected behavior
`setConnectTimeout(0)` should be accepted. In 4.5.27 and 5.0.12,
`ClientOptionsBase#setConnectTimeout(int)` uses `connectTimeout < 0` and treats
0 as "no timeout" (Netty falls back to the OS default for
`CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MILLIS`).

## Actual behavior
`Duration.ZERO` is rejected with a message that says it should be allowed.
There is no public API in 5.1 to express "no/infinite connect timeout" —
the `Duration` cannot be `null`, and zero is now forbidden.

## Code reference
[`TcpClientConfig.java:117-122` @ 5.1.0](https://github.com/eclipse-vertx/vert.x/blob/5.1.0/vertx-core/src/main/java/io/vertx/core/net/TcpClientConfig.java#L117-L122):
```java
public TcpClientConfig setConnectTimeout(Duration connectTimeout) {
  if (connectTimeout.isNegative() || connectTimeout.isZero()) {
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("connectTimeout must be >= 0");
  }
  this.connectTimeout = connectTimeout;
  return this;
}
```

For comparison, [`ClientOptionsBase.java:140-146` @ 5.0.12](https://github.com/eclipse-vertx/vert.x/blob/5.0.12/vertx-core/src/main/java/io/vertx/core/net/ClientOptionsBase.java#L140-L146):
```java
public ClientOptionsBase setConnectTimeout(int connectTimeout) {
  if (connectTimeout < 0) {
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("connectTimeout must be >= 0");
  }
  this.connectTimeout = connectTimeout;
  return this;
}
```

## Downstream impact
Surfaced in [fabric8io/kubernetes-client#7875](https://github.com/fabric8io/kubernetes-client/pull/7875), the bump from 5.0.12 to 5.1.0. We expose
`HttpClient.Builder#connectTimeout(long, TimeUnit)` and have always accepted
`0` as "no timeout"; every other backend (JDK HttpClient, OkHttp, Jetty, Vert.x
4.x, Vert.x 5.0.x) honors that. The PR is in draft pending clarification here.

## Questions
1. Is the new validation intentional? If yes, the message should be `> 0` and the migration notes should call out the lost "no timeout" sentinel.
2. If unintentional, dropping `isZero()` from the predicate would restore prior behavior.
3. If 0 is no longer the supported "no timeout" sentinel, what is?

