Datadog is parsing the result of `HttpServerRequest#absoluteURI()` as a `URI`. This succeeds but apparently within URI an exception is generated, caught and handled for this port=-1 case around https://github.com/openjdk/jdk/blob/004d0ecf8693961455f4fe75cf0232aa6eead307/src/java.base/share/classes/java/net/URI.java#L3277

While the explicit `:-1` added for the port isn't invalid as part of the URI, it seems redundant, and triggering this exception internally is unnecessary overhead for that redundancy.

To see that the explicit `-1` port generates an exception despite succeeding, you can run this with jshell:

```java
import jdk.jfr.Recording;
import jdk.jfr.consumer.RecordedClass;
import jdk.jfr.consumer.RecordingFile;
import java.net.URI;
import java.nio.file.Files;

void countURISyntaxExceptions(String uriStr) throws Exception {
    var tmpFile = Files.createTempFile("jfr-uri-test", ".jfr");
    try {
        var rec = new Recording();
        rec.enable("jdk.JavaExceptionThrow").withStackTrace();
        rec.start();
        try { URI.create(uriStr); } catch (Exception ignore) {}
        rec.stop();
        rec.dump(tmpFile);
        long count = RecordingFile.readAllEvents(tmpFile).stream()
            .filter(e -> {
                Object cls = e.getValue("thrownClass");
                return cls instanceof RecordedClass rc && rc.getName().equals("java.net.URISyntaxException");
            })
            .count();
        System.out.printf("%-42s -> %d URISyntaxException(s) created%n", "\"" + uriStr + "\"", count);
    } finally {
        Files.deleteIfExists(tmpFile);
    }
}

countURISyntaxExceptions("http://example.com:-1/path");
countURISyntaxExceptions("http://example.com/path");
```

This ultimately seems to arise from an operator precedence quirk in `HttpUtils.absoluteURI` that I've got a separate test/fix I'll PR shortly.

